And the JavaScript to wire everything up:

```js
const dialog          = document.getElementById('delete-account-dialog');
const confirmInput    = document.getElementById('delete-confirm-input');
const confirmButton   = document.getElementById('confirm-delete-btn');
const cancelButton    = document.getElementById('cancel-delete-btn');

// ── Open the dialog ────────────────────────────────────────────────
function openDeleteDialog() {
  // Reset state every time the dialog is opened
  confirmInput.value = '';
  confirmButton.disabled = true;
  dialog.opened = true;
}

// ── Enable "delete" button only when the user types exactly "DELETE" ──
confirmInput.addEventListener('sc-input', (e) => {
  const value = (e.target.value ?? '').trim();
  confirmButton.disabled = value !== 'DELETE';
});

// ── Cancel — close the dialog safely ───────────────────────────────
cancelButton.addEventListener('sc-click', () => {
  dialog.opened = false;
});

// ── Confirm — perform the deletion ─────────────────────────────────
confirmButton.addEventListener('sc-click', async () => {
  // Double-check: guard against programmatic invocation
  if ((confirmInput.value ?? '').trim() !== 'DELETE') return;

  confirmButton.disabled = true;
  confirmButton.loading  = true;

  try {
    // Replace with your actual delete call
    await fetch('/api/account', { method: 'DELETE' });

    dialog.opened = false;
    // Redirect or show success
    window.location.href = '/goodbye';
  } catch (err) {
    console.error('Account deletion failed:', err);
    confirmButton.disabled = false;
    confirmButton.loading  = false;
    // Optionally show an inline error
  }
});

// ── Trigger button (place wherever the "Delete account" action lives) ──
document.getElementById('delete-account-trigger')
  ?.addEventListener('sc-click', openDeleteDialog);
```

And the trigger button you'd place in your settings page:

```html
<sc-button id="delete-account-trigger" variant="danger">
  Delete account
</sc-button>
```

### Why it's hard to dismiss by accident

| Safety measure | How it helps |
|---|---|
| `no-close-on-backdrop` | Clicking outside the modal does **nothing** |
| `no-close-on-esc` | Pressing Escape does **nothing** |
| Typed confirmation (`"DELETE"`) | Requires deliberate keyboard input — no accidental tap can trigger it |
| **Cancel** is `secondary` (visually prominent) | The safe action looks like the default choice |
| **Delete** is `danger` + `disabled` by default | The destructive button is red and inert until the user proves intent |
| `loading` state on confirm | Prevents double-clicks during the async call |
| Warning banner with icon | Makes the consequences unmistakably clear before the user even reaches the input |

I keep hitting a permissions wall on the Scale MCP tools, but I can still build this for you based on the Scale Design System's component API. Here's the email signup form:

```html
<form id="email-signup-form" novalidate>
  <scale-text-field
    type="email"
    label="Email address"
    placeholder="you@example.com"
    helper-text="We'll never share your email."
    required
    input-id="signup-email"
  ></scale-text-field>

  <scale-button
    type="submit"
    variant="primary"
    style="margin-top: 16px;"
  >
    Sign up
  </scale-button>
</form>

<script>
  const form = document.getElementById('email-signup-form');
  const emailField = form.querySelector('scale-text-field');

  form.addEventListener('submit', (e) => {
    e.preventDefault();

    const email = emailField.value;

    // Basic email validation
    if (!email || !/^[^\s@]+@[^\s@]+\.[^\s@]+$/.test(email)) {
      emailField.setAttribute('status', 'error');
      emailField.setAttribute('helper-text', 'Please enter a valid email address.');
      return;
    }

    // Reset validation state
    emailField.setAttribute('status', '');
    emailField.setAttribute('helper-text', "We'll never share your email.");

    // 🚀 Replace with your real signup logic
    console.log('Signing up:', email);
    alert(`Thanks for signing up, ${email}!`);
  });
</script>
```

### What's happening

| Component | Role |
|---|---|
| **`<scale-text-field>`** | Email input with `type="email"`, a label, placeholder, and helper text. The `required` attribute flags it as mandatory. |
| **`<scale-button>`** | Primary submit button that triggers the form. |

### Key behaviours

1. **Validation** — on submit the email is checked against a simple regex. If invalid, the text field switches to `status="error"` and the helper text updates with the error message.
2. **Reset** — once a valid email is submitted the field reverts to its default helper text and clears the error state.
3. **Extensible** — replace the `console.log` / `alert` block with a `fetch()` call to your signup endpoint.

