And here's a working example with a simulated upload that updates the bar dynamically:

```html
<div style="max-width: 480px; font-family: var(--scl-font-family-body);">

  <!-- Progress bar bound to the current upload percentage -->
  <sc-progress-bar
    id="upload-bar"
    percentage="0"
    label="Uploading document.pdf"
    show-status
  ></sc-progress-bar>

  <br />
  <sc-button id="upload-btn" variant="primary">Start Upload</sc-button>
</div>

<script>
  const bar = document.getElementById('upload-bar');
  const btn = document.getElementById('upload-btn');
  const totalBytes = 5_242_880; // 5 MB

  function formatBytes(bytes) {
    return (bytes / 1_048_576).toFixed(1) + ' MB';
  }

  btn.addEventListener('click', () => {
    btn.disabled = true;
    let uploaded = 0;

    const interval = setInterval(() => {
      // Simulate a chunk arriving
      uploaded += Math.floor(Math.random() * 400_000) + 100_000;
      if (uploaded > totalBytes) uploaded = totalBytes;

      const pct = Math.round((uploaded / totalBytes) * 100);

      // Update the progress bar
      bar.percentage = pct;
      bar.customDescription = `${pct} % — ${formatBytes(uploaded)} of ${formatBytes(totalBytes)}`;

      if (pct >= 100) {
        clearInterval(interval);
        bar.label = 'Upload complete!';
        bar.customDescription = `${formatBytes(totalBytes)} uploaded`;
        btn.disabled = false;
      }
    }, 300);
  });
</script>
```

### What's happening

| Attribute / Property    | Purpose                                                        |
|-------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------|
| `percentage`            | Fills the bar (0–100)                                          |
| `label`                 | Heading text above the bar (e.g. the file name)                |
| `show-status`           | Displays the percentage number alongside the bar               |
| `custom-description`    | Secondary line — used here to show bytes transferred vs. total |

The script simulates chunked data arriving every 300 ms, recalculates the percentage, and writes it back to the `sc-progress-bar`. In a real app you'd replace the `setInterval` with your `fetch`/`XMLHttpRequest` upload progress callback:

```js
const xhr = new XMLHttpRequest();
xhr.upload.addEventListener('progress', (e) => {
  if (e.lengthComputable) {
    const pct = Math.round((e.loaded / e.total) * 100);
    bar.percentage = pct;
    bar.customDescription = `${pct} % — ${formatBytes(e.loaded)} of ${formatBytes(e.total)}`;
  }
});
```

