Here's how to animate an expanding panel consistent with the Scale Design System:

---

### Option 1 — Use the built-in `sc-collapsible` (recommended)

The Scale DS ships with `<sc-collapsible>`, which already has a smooth expand/collapse animation built in:

```html
<sc-collapsible label="Panel Title">
  <p>Your expandable content goes here.</p>
</sc-collapsible>
```

The animation is baked in and system-consistent — no extra CSS needed.

---

### Option 2 — Custom panel with Scale-consistent animation

If you need a custom panel (e.g. a sidebar or drawer), mirror the motion tokens that Scale DS uses internally:

```css
/*
 * Scale DS motion conventions:
 *   duration: 200ms–250ms for small UI, 300ms for larger panels
 *   easing:   cubic-bezier(0.4, 0, 0.2, 1)  (standard "ease-in-out" from Material/Telekom)
 *   property: max-height + opacity for reveal, transform for slides
 */

.panel {
  overflow: hidden;
  max-height: 0;
  opacity: 0;
  transition:
    max-height 250ms cubic-bezier(0.4, 0, 0.2, 1),
    opacity 200ms cubic-bezier(0.4, 0, 0.2, 1);
  /* use Scale spacing tokens for padding */
  padding-inline: var(--scl-spacing-16);
}

.panel[open] {
  max-height: 500px; /* generous upper bound; adjust to content */
  opacity: 1;
  padding-block: var(--scl-spacing-16);
}
```

### With a toggle trigger using Scale components:

```html
<sc-button id="toggle-btn" variant="secondary" size="small">
  Open Panel
</sc-button>

<div class="panel" id="my-panel">
  <p>Expanding content lives here.</p>
</div>

<script>
  const btn = document.getElementById('toggle-btn');
  const panel = document.getElementById('my-panel');

  btn.addEventListener('click', () => {
    const isOpen = panel.hasAttribute('open');
    if (isOpen) {
      panel.removeAttribute('open');
      btn.textContent = 'Open Panel';
    } else {
      panel.setAttribute('open', '');
      btn.textContent = 'Close Panel';
    }
  });
</script>
```

---

### Option 3 — Robust height animation (auto-height via JS)

The `max-height` trick can feel laggy if the bound is too far from the real content height. For a pixel-perfect version:

```js
function togglePanel(panelEl) {
  if (panelEl.hasAttribute('open')) {
    // Collapse: animate from current height → 0
    const height = panelEl.scrollHeight;
    panelEl.style.maxHeight = `${height}px`;
    // Force reflow so the browser registers the starting value
    panelEl.offsetHeight;
    panelEl.style.maxHeight = '0';
    panelEl.style.opacity = '0';

    panelEl.addEventListener('transitionend', () => {
      panelEl.removeAttribute('open');
      panelEl.style.maxHeight = '';
    }, { once: true });
  } else {
    // Expand: animate from 0 → scrollHeight
    panelEl.setAttribute('open', '');
    panelEl.style.maxHeight = '0';
    panelEl.offsetHeight; // reflow
    panelEl.style.maxHeight = `${panelEl.scrollHeight}px`;
    panelEl.style.opacity = '1';

    panelEl.addEventListener('transitionend', () => {
      panelEl.style.maxHeight = 'none'; // allow dynamic content
    }, { once: true });
  }
}
```

---

### Key values to stay consistent with Scale DS

| Concern | Value | Why |
|---|---|---|
| **Duration** | `250ms` | Matches Scale's internal collapse/expand timing |
| **Easing** | `cubic-bezier(0.4, 0, 0.2, 1)` | Standard easing across Scale components |
| **Reduced motion** | Respect `prefers-reduced-motion` | Scale DS does this internally |
| **Spacing** | `--scl-spacing-*` tokens | Keeps padding/gaps aligned with the system |

```css
/* Always respect reduced-motion preferences, as Scale DS does */
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  .panel {
    transition-duration: 0ms;
  }
}
```

**Recommendation:** Prefer `<sc-collapsible>` (Option 1) whenever possible — it handles animation, accessibility (`aria-expanded`, keyboard support), and motion preferences out of the box. Use the custom approaches only when you need a non-standard layout like a sliding drawer or sidebar.

