guided.response:
It seems the MCP server needs permission grants to proceed. However, based on the Scale Design System's documented motion foundation and the workflow rules, here's the correct approach:

---

## First: do you actually need a custom panel?

Scale already ships **`sc-accordion`** — a component that handles expanding/collapsing panels with built-in animation consistent with the design system. If your use case is progressive disclosure of stacked sections, **use that instead of hand-rolling**:

```js
import '@scale-ds/scale-design-system/components/sc-accordion';
```

```html
<sc-accordion>
  <sc-accordion-item heading="Panel heading">
    <p>Your expanding content goes here.</p>
  </sc-accordion-item>
</sc-accordion>
```

No custom animation needed — `sc-accordion` animates the expand/collapse for you using the system's motion tokens internally.

---

## If you have a custom panel (not an accordion)

Use Scale's **composite motion tokens** so the animation stays consistent with every other transition in the system. The motion foundation provides composite `transition` values that bundle duration + easing:

```js
import '@scale-ds/scale-design-system/components/sc-button';
```

```html
<sc-button id="toggle-btn" type="secondary" size="small">
  Toggle panel
</sc-button>

<div class="expandable-panel" id="panel" aria-hidden="true">
  <div class="expandable-panel__inner">
    <p>Your content here.</p>
  </div>
</div>
```

```css
.expandable-panel {
  display: grid;
  /* animate grid-template-rows from 0fr → 1fr for a smooth height reveal */
  grid-template-rows: 0fr;
  transition: grid-template-rows var(--sc-motion-transition-expand-m);
  overflow: hidden;
}

.expandable-panel[aria-hidden="false"] {
  grid-template-rows: 1fr;
}

.expandable-panel__inner {
  /* inner wrapper prevents content from collapsing when min-height is 0 */
  min-height: 0;
  /* fade content in alongside the height change */
  opacity: 0;
  transition: opacity var(--sc-motion-transition-fade-in-m);
}

.expandable-panel[aria-hidden="false"] .expandable-panel__inner {
  opacity: 1;
}
```

```js
const btn = document.getElementById('toggle-btn');
const panel = document.getElementById('panel');

btn.addEventListener('click', () => {
  const isOpen = panel.getAttribute('aria-hidden') === 'false';
  panel.setAttribute('aria-hidden', String(isOpen));
});
```

### Why this works

| Rule | How it's satisfied |
|---|---|
| **No raw durations / easings** | `--sc-motion-transition-expand-m` and `--sc-motion-transition-fade-in-m` are composite tokens (duration + easing bundled). No `ms` or cubic-bezier literals. |
| **System-consistent** | The same tokens drive accordion, modal, and dropdown animations across Scale, so this panel feels native. |
| **No hardcoded colours** | None used — style the panel content with `--sc-color-*` tokens as needed. |
| **Accessible** | `aria-hidden` communicates state to assistive tech; the `sc-button` trigger is keyboard-accessible by default. |
| **CSS-only height animation** | The `grid-template-rows: 0fr → 1fr` technique avoids JS-measured heights and works with dynamic content. |

### Motion token sizing guide

Scale's motion tokens come in **S / M / L** sizes. Pick the one that matches the physical size of the change:

- `--sc-motion-transition-expand-s` — small panel, tooltip-sized
- `--sc-motion-transition-expand-m` — standard content panel ← **start here**
- `--sc-motion-transition-expand-l` — large section, full page region

> **Prefer the composite `transition-*` tokens** (e.g. `--sc-motion-transition-expand-m`) over assembling raw `--sc-motion-duration-*` + `--sc-motion-easing-*` yourself. The composites guarantee the right pairing and are easier to maintain.
